ptions</h2><div class=\"sub\">Active subscriptions right now &middot; test accounts left out</div><div class=\"metrics\"><div class=\"metric hero\"><div class=\"k\">Total GLP-1 Subscriptions</div><div class=\"v\">0</div>about $0 a month, estimated</div><div class=\"metric\"><div class=\"k\">Compounded tirzepatide</div><div class=\"v\">0</div>about $0 a month, estimated</div><div class=\"metric\"><div class=\"k\">Compounded semaglutide</div><div class=\"v\">0</div>about $0 a month, estimated</div></div><p class=\"note\">The monthly figure is an estimate, not money billed. It takes each plan's price, scales it to a 30 day month (a plan billed every 28 days counts 30/28 of its price) and takes off any discount.</p></section>","lab_html":"<h3 class=\"mini\">Which lab filled the order</h3><div class=\"sub\">Every order sent to a lab, back to Aug 17</div><table class=\"labs\"><thead><tr><th>Lab</th><th>Total orders</th><th>First order</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Quest</td><td class=\"amt\">773</td><td class=\"tm\">Aug 17</td></tr><tr><td>Labcorp</td><td class=\"amt\">54</td><td class=\"tm\">Sep 18</td></tr><tr><td>BioReference</td><td class=\"amt\">27</td><td class=\"tm\">Sep 18</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">Labcorp and BioReference started much later than Quest, so a small number here reflects how new they are, not weak demand.</p>","mix_days":8,"pace_html":"<section><h2>Are we on pace today?</h2><div class=\"sub\">Orders so far today against a normal day at the same hour &middot; Pacific</div><div class=\"metrics\"><div class=\"metric hero\"><div class=\"k\">Orders so far</div><div class=\"v\">29</div><span class=\"delta up\">\u25b2 145%</span> vs 11.8 by this hour</div><div class=\"metric\"><div class=\"k\">On track to finish</div><div class=\"v\">29</div>about $4,768</div><div class=\"metric\"><div class=\"k\">A normal full day</div><div class=\"v\">12.0</div>$1,779 in revenue</div></div><div class=\"chartwrap short\"><canvas id=\"pace_chart\"></canvas></div><p class=\"note\">The red line is today and it stops at the current hour. The dashed line is where a normal day sits at each hour, averaged over the 63 previous days we have. Right now we are <b>ahead of</b> that pace. The finishing figure assumes the rest of today follows the same shape as those days, which off 63 days of history is a rough guide, not a forecast.</p></section>","pace":{"labels":["12a","1a","2a","3a","4a","5a","6a","7a","8a","9a","10a","11a","12p","1p","2p","3p","4p","5p","6p","7p","8p","9p","10p","11p"],"today":[1,1,1,2,3,3,4,6,7,8,10,12,15,16,19,20,21,23,24,26,27,29,29],"avg":[0.11,0.19,0.25,0.38,0.54,1.0,1.62,2.49,3.37,4.41,5.21,6.16,7.05,7.79,8.59,9.13,9.68,10.05,10.33,10.83,11.14,11.57,11.84,12.02],"now_h":22},"traffic_html":"<section><h2>Is traffic normal today?</h2><div class=\"sub\">Too little of today has been counted to say yet</div><p class=\"note\">Analytics has finished counting 0 hours of today so far, and this needs 4 finished hours before it can compare anything. It fills in as the day goes on.</p></section>","traffic":{"ready":false},"impact_html":"<section><h2>Did our changes work?</h2><div class=\"sub\">One verdict per change, each measured on the number it should actually move</div><div class=\"vc\"><div class=\"vh\"><b>STD pricing page rebuilt</b><span class=\"flag funnel\">funnel</span><span class=\"vd\">Aug 26 &middot; 9:57 AM</span></div><p class=\"note\">Our main STD pricing page was rebuilt to Allen's new design, mobile first, with the trust rail carrying the Trustpilot rating and CLIA badge, new copy throughout, and every CTA going straight to the cart.</p><div class=\"vh\"><span class=\"vb early\">Waiting for data</span><span class=\"vd\">measured on /std-test-pricing/</span></div><p class=\"note\">Not enough data on one side of this change yet, measured on /std-test-pricing/.</p></div><div class=\"vc\"><div class=\"vh\"><b>Place My Order double-click fix</b><span class=\"flag funnel\">funnel</span><span class=\"vd\">Aug 21 &middot; 8:30 AM</span></div><p class=\"note\">The button stayed live after the first click, so a second click created another Stripe invoice for the same order. Five of the first 19 paid orders went through two or three invoices.</p><div class=\"vh\"><span class=\"vb early\">Waiting for data</span><span class=\"vd\">measured on checkout</span></div><p class=\"note\">Not enough data on one side of this change yet, measured on checkout.</p></div><div class=\"vc\"><div class=\"vh\"><b>Checkout stops re-asking for a lab</b><span class=\"flag funnel\">funnel</span><span class=\"vd\">Aug 21 &middot; 8:50 AM</span></div><p class=\"note\">Checkout showed the whole Test Centers module again even when the shopper had already picked a location, worst on mobile. It now shows the chosen lab with a single Change Location button.</p><div class=\"vh\"><span class=\"vb early\">Waiting for data</span><span class=\"vd\">measured on checkout</span></div><p class=\"note\">Not enough data on one side of this change yet, measured on checkout.</p></div><div class=\"vc\"><div class=\"vh\"><b>GEO button to pricing page</b><span class=\"flag funnel\">funnel</span><span class=\"vd\">Aug 19 &middot; morning</span></div><p class=\"note\">Clicking Choose This Location on an STD city page now goes to the STD pricing page instead of the city list. Raised and fixed the same day.</p><div class=\"vh\"><span class=\"vb early\">Waiting for data</span><span class=\"vd\">measured on /std-testing/</span></div><p class=\"note\">Not enough data on one side of this change yet, measured on /std-testing/.</p></div><div class=\"vc\"><div class=\"vh\"><b>Monetization live</b><span class=\"flag launch\">launch</span><span class=\"vd\">Aug 18 &middot; 12:00 AM</span></div><p class=\"note\">Checkout opened on testing.com. First customer order came through at 1:03 AM from Google organic.</p><p class=\"note\">This is the starting line, so there is nothing before it to compare against.</p></div><h3 class=\"mini\">Cart rate since launch</h3><div class=\"chartwrap\"><canvas id=\"chg_chart\"></canvas></div><p class=\"note\">Carts per 1,000 organic visits, split by the section each change affected, with the whole site dashed for reference. Only changes measured on cart rate appear here. Checkout changes move a different number and are judged on their own cards above, and a change to a page people click through to rather than land on gets its own chart below. A section line is the only place a section-level change can show up: STD GEO is about a tenth of our visits, so a big move there barely dents the sitewide line. Carts are the measure rather than orders because orders run single digits a day and cannot separate signal from noise for weeks. 31 days of data so far.</p><h3 class=\"mini\">Cart rate on /std-test-pricing/</h3><div class=\"chartwrap\"><canvas id=\"chg_page_chart\"></canvas></div><p class=\"note\">Share of organic visits to /std-test-pricing/ that add something to the cart. Counted on the page people <b>viewed</b>, not the page they landed on, which is why this is a separate chart and not another line above. Almost nobody arrives on the pricing page from search, they click through to it, so its landing-page numbers run single digits a day while it fires more than half of every cart on the site. One visit counts once here however many tests it adds, so this is a share of people rather than a count of clicks. The two charts count different groups of people and their heights do not compare.</p><p class=\"note\">Changes are recorded by hand, big ones only, and each needs the right <code>scope</code> and <code>metric</code>. To add one, edit <code>changes.json</code>.</p></section>","impact":{"labels":["Sep 20","Sep 21","Sep 22","Sep 23","Sep 24","Sep 25","Sep 26","Sep 27","Sep 28","Sep 29","Sep 30","Oct 1","Oct 2","Oct 3","Oct 4","Oct 5","Oct 6","Oct 7","Oct 8","Oct 9","Oct 10","Oct 11","Oct 12","Oct 13","Oct 14","Oct 15","Oct 16","Oct 17","Oct 18","Oct 19","Oct 2