 not recording checkout correctly yet: it logged\n    <b>15 payment steps</b> today against\n    <b>29 real paid orders</b>, and our purchase event sends no order value,\n    so Analytics reports <b>$0 revenue</b> no matter what sells. Use it to see which\n    pages bring people in, never to count orders or money.\n  </div>\n  <h3 class=\"mini\">Traffic and carts by page type</h3>\n  <table class=\"attr6\"><thead><tr><th>Page type</th><th>Engaged visits</th><th>Carts</th>\n  <th>Cart rate today</th><th>Usual</th><th>vs usual</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody><tr class=\"hot\"><td>/tests/</td><td class=\"amt\">1,591</td><td class=\"amt\">33</td><td class=\"amt\">2.07%</td><td class=\"amt\">1.82%</td><td class=\"amt\"><span style=\"color:#9aa1ac\">normal</span></td></tr><tr><td>/treatments/</td><td class=\"amt\">929</td><td class=\"amt\">4</td><td class=\"amt\">0.43%</td><td class=\"amt\"><span style=\"color:#9aa1ac\">no data</span></td><td class=\"amt\">&mdash;</td></tr><tr class=\"hot\"><td>/std-testing/</td><td class=\"amt\">493</td><td class=\"amt\">65</td><td class=\"amt\">13.18%</td><td class=\"amt\">12.99%</td><td class=\"amt\"><span style=\"color:#9aa1ac\">normal</span></td></tr><tr><td>/articles/</td><td class=\"amt\">350</td><td class=\"amt\">1</td><td class=\"amt\">0.29%</td><td class=\"amt\">0.06%</td><td class=\"amt\"><span style=\"color:#9aa1ac\">too few</span></td></tr><tr class=\"hot\"><td>Everything else</td><td class=\"amt\">336</td><td class=\"amt\">4</td><td class=\"amt\">1.19%</td><td class=\"amt\">0.65%</td><td class=\"amt\"><span style=\"color:#9aa1ac\">normal</span></td></tr><tr class=\"hot\"><td>/blood-testing/</td><td class=\"amt\">39</td><td class=\"amt\">2</td><td class=\"amt\">5.13%</td><td class=\"amt\">2.02%</td><td class=\"amt\"><span style=\"color:#9aa1ac\">too few</span></td></tr><tr class=\"hot\"><td>Homepage</td><td class=\"amt\">38</td><td class=\"amt\">8</td><td class=\"amt\">21.05%</td><td class=\"amt\">7.22%</td><td class=\"amt\"><span class=\"vb good\">+192%</span></td></tr><tr><td>/std-test-pricing</td><td class=\"amt\">3</td><td class=\"amt\">0</td><td class=\"amt\">0.00%</td><td class=\"amt\">23.08%</td><td class=\"amt\"><span style=\"color:#9aa1ac\">too few</span></td></tr><tr><td>/find-a-lab</td><td class=\"amt\">2</td><td class=\"amt\">0</td><td class=\"amt\">0.00%</td><td class=\"amt\">22.22%</td><td class=\"amt\"><span style=\"color:#9aa1ac\">too few</span></td></tr></tbody></table>\n  <p class=\"note\"><b>Usual</b> is this page type's cart rate across the last 14 whole days (2026-10-06 to 2026-10-19), with any day the site was not recording carts left out. <b>vs usual</b> only prints a number when today is far enough from usual to mean something. A page type with few carts swings a long way on chance alone, so a small section has to move much further than a big one before it counts. Everything else says <b>normal</b>. Today is still part of a day, so read this as a rate comparison, never as a count. Both rate columns count cart presses rather than people, so a page type where one person adds several tests can read above 100%: the pricing page lists panels side by side and usually does. \"Everything else\" is every landing page outside the types named above, mostly the category and guide pages, and its usual rate is worked out by subtraction because the history cannot ask for it directly. Direct visits and their carts are left out of every column here, today and usual alike, because a data center that presses the cart button on /tests/ pages dozens of times a day arrives as Direct.</p>\n  <h3 class=\"mini\">By how people arrived</h3>\n  <table class=\"attr\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Engaged visits</th><th>Carts</th><th>Cart rate</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody><tr><td>Organic Search</td><td class=\"amt\">2,622</td><td class=\"amt\">79</td><td class=\"amt\">3.01%</td></tr><tr><td>Direct</td><td class=\"amt\">2,250</td><td class=\"amt\">41</td><td class=\"amt\">1.82%</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-network</td><td class=\"amt\">1,005</td><td class=\"amt\">34</td><td class=\"amt\">3.38%</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td class=\"amt\">68</td><td class=\"amt\">1</td><td class=\"amt\">1.47%</td></tr><tr><td>Unassigned</td><td class=\"amt\">25</td><td class=\"amt\">2</td><td class=\"amt\">8.00%</td></tr></tbody></table>\n  <h3 class=\"mini\">By device</h3>\n  <table class=\"attr\"><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Engaged visits</th><th>Carts</th><th>Cart rate</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody><tr><td>Mobile</td><td class=\"amt\">4,402</td><td class=\"amt\">109</td><td class=\"amt\">2.48%</td></tr><tr><td>Desktop</td><td class=\"amt\">1,494</td><td class=\"amt\">48</td><td class=\"amt\">3.21%</td></tr><tr><td>Tablet</td><td class=\"amt\">102</td><td class=\"amt\">1</td><td class=\"amt\">0.98%</td></tr></tbody></table>\n  <h3 class=\"mini\">Which page the cart button is actually pressed on</h3><table class=\"attr2\"><thead><tr><th>Page</th><th>Views today</th><th>Carts</th><th>Cart rate today</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>/std-test-pricing/</td><td class=\"amt\">377</td><td class=\"amt\">63</td><td class=\"amt\">14.32%</td></tr><tr><td>/tests/complete-blood-count-cbc/</td><td class=\"amt\">31</td><td class=\"amt\">13</td><td class=\"amt\">38.71%</td></tr><tr><td>/</td><td class=\"amt\">1,665</td><td class=\"amt\">11</td><td class=\"amt\">0.42%</td></tr><tr><td>/tests/fasting-insulin-test/</td><td class=\"amt\">43</td><td class=\"amt\">8</td><td class=\"amt\">18.60%</td></tr><tr><td>/tests/</td><td class=\"amt\">594</td><td class=\"amt\">4</td><td class=\"amt\">0.67%</td></tr><tr><td>/tests/allergy-testing/</td><td class=\"amt\">29</td><td class=\"amt\">4</td><td class=\"amt\">6.90%</td></tr><tr><td>/std-testing/herpes-1-2-test/</td><td class=\"amt\">12</td><td class=\"amt\">3</td><td class=\"amt\">25.00%</td></tr><tr><td>/tests/thyroid-tests/</td><td class=\"amt\">16</td><td class=\"amt\">3</td><td class=\"amt\">12.50%</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">This cart rate is counted differently from the three tables above, and the two are not comparable. Here it is the share of people who viewed that page and added something to the cart, however they arrived. Above, it is cart presses divided by visits that STARTED on that kind of page. A page can convert people it did not bring in, so a page can rank high here and low there.</p>\n  <p class=\"note\"><b>Visits above are engaged sessions</b>, meaning someone actually read\n  the page. Today 12,159 of 18,167 US\n  sessions were non-engaged, almost all of it datacenter crawler traffic from Google Cloud\n  and AWS that loads a page for two seconds and leaves. Counting those would roughly\n  quadruple the denominator and make every rate here look four times worse than reality.</p>\n</section>","notice_html":"","daily":[{"date":"2026-09-20","partial":false,"organic_visits":2898,"engaged_sessions":4169,"checkout_visits":71,"org_checkout_visits":48,"carts":94,"reached_checkout":29},{"date":"2026-09-21","partial":false,"organic_visits":4359,"engaged_sessions":6774,"checkout_visits":121,"org_checkout_visits":84,"carts":180,"reached_checkout":74},{"date":"2026-09-22","partial":false,"organic_visits":4227,"engaged_sessions":7055,"checkout_visits":131,"org_checkout_visits":73,"carts":173,"reached_checkout":83},{"date":"2026-09-23","partial":false,"organic_visits":4417,"engaged_sessions":7180,"checkout_visits":145,"org_checkout_visits":89,"carts":198,"reached_checkout":71},{"date":"2026-09-24","partial":false,"organic_visits":4272,"engaged_sessions":6813,"checkout_visits":118,"org_checkout_visits":82,"carts":165,"reached_checkout":57},{"date":"2026-09-25","partial":false,"organic_visits":3525,"engaged_sessions":5636,"checkout_visits":115,"org_checkout_visits":68,"carts":141,"reached_checkout":56},{"date":"2026-09-26","partial":false,"organic_visits":2647,"engaged_sessions":4090,"checkout_visits":67,"org_checkout_visits":47,"carts":85,"reached_checkout":25},{"date":"2026-09-27","partial":fals